index 00000000000..2f0d212708c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/index.md
@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+---
+title: CFFI
+---
+
+# CFFI
+
+CFFI is an engine module that lets GDScript call C functions in shared libraries directly, and
+call back from C into GDScript. It wraps libffi, so the calling convention, struct layout and
+variadic arguments are handled for you.
+
+It is always available: no autoload, no plugin, no `preload`. `CFFI` is an engine singleton, and
+`CFFILibraryHandle`, `CFFIFunction`, `CFFIType`, `CFFIPointer` and the rest are engine classes.
+
+## Quick start
+
+```gdscript
+var lib := CFFI.open("libc.so.6")                       # Linux
+# var lib := CFFI.open("/usr/lib/libSystem.B.dylib")     # macOS
+# var lib := CFFI.open("msvcrt.dll")                     # Windows
+
+var strlen: CFFIFunction = lib.get_function("strlen", "size_t", ["char*"])
+var text := CFFI.null_terminated_utf8_buffer("hello")
+
+print(strlen.invokev([text]))   # 5
+```
+
+## Pages
+
+| page | covers |
+|---|---|
+| [CFFI](cffi.md) | opening libraries, finding types, structs, callbacks, memory helpers |
+| [Type strings](type-strings.md) | how types are written: `"int"`, `"char*"`, `"float[3]"`, `"const char**"` |
+| [CFFILibraryHandle](library-handle.md) | `get_function`, `get_global` |
+| [CFFIFunction](function.md) | `invokev` |
+| [CFFIType](types.md) | type objects, `alloc`, structs, arrays, pointer types |
+| [CFFIPointer](pointer.md) | reading and writing memory, strings, field access |
+| [CFFISpan](span.md) | bounded views over memory |
+| [CFFICallableFunction](callable-function.md) | passing a GDScript Callable to C |
+| [StreamPeerCFFIPointer](stream-peer.md) | reading and writing through `StreamPeer` |
+| [Examples](examples.md) | complete worked examples |
+
+## Types
+
+Arguments to `invokev` and values returned from it are converted between GDScript `Variant`s and C
+types according to the declared type strings. Passing the wrong GDScript type for a declared C type
+is a runtime error rather than a silent truncation.
diff --git a/docs/library-handle.md b/docs/library-handle.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000000..a8943469ece
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/library-handle.md
@@ -0,0 +1,50 @@
+---
+title: CFFILibraryHandle
+---
+
+# CFFILibraryHandle
+
+A loaded shared library. Returned by [CFFI.open](cffi.md#open); declares nothing by itself.
+
+```gdscript
+var libm := CFFI.open("libm.so.6")
+var pow: CFFIFunction = libm.get_function("pow", "double", ["double", "double"])
+```
+
+## get_function
+
+```
+CFFIFunction get_function(String name, Variant return_type, Array argument_types = [], bool is_variadic = false)
+```
+
+Looks up an exported symbol and builds a callable from it. Returns `null` if the symbol does not
+exist, which is the cheapest way to feature-detect at runtime.
+
+- `name` — the exported symbol name, exactly as C exports it.
+- `return_type` — a [type string](type-strings.md) or `CFFIType`.
+- `argument_types` — one entry per parameter, in order.
+- `is_variadic` — set `true` when the C function takes `...`; pass the fixed parameters in
+  `argument_types` and the rest in the `invokev` argument list.
+
+```gdscript
+var printf := libc.get_function("printf", "int", ["char*"], true)
+printf.invokev([CFFI.null_terminated_utf8_buffer("%d %s\n"), 42,
+                CFFI.null_terminated_utf8_buffer("items")])
+```
+
+## get_global
+
+```
+CFFIPointer get_global(String name, Variant type)
+```
+
+Returns a pointer to an exported global variable, so you can read and write it.
+
+```gdscript
+var errno := libc.get_global("errno", "int")
+if errno != null:
+    print(errno.get_value())
+    errno.set_value(0)
+```
+
+The pointer refers to the library's storage; it stays valid while the library is loaded.
diff --git a/docs/pointer.md b/docs/pointer.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000000..32f158c5fca
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/pointer.md
@@ -0,0 +1,142 @@
+---
+title: CFFIPointer
+---
+
+# CFFIPointer
+
+A pointer into C memory. Returned by functions with pointer return types, by `CFFIType.alloc`, by
+`CFFISpan.get_data` and `CFFISpan.get_pointer`, and by `CFFI.get_pointer`.
+
+A `CFFIPointer` does not own memory unless it is a [CFFIOwnedValue](#cffiownedvalue) — the two
+subclasses that own memory are `CFFIOwnedValue` (one value) and
+[CFFIOwnedArray](span.md#cffiownedarray) (an array).
+
+## Reading and writing
+
+```
+Variant get_value(int index = 0)
+void    set_value(Variant value, int index = 0)
+```
+
+`index` counts elements of the pointer's element type, not bytes.
+
+```gdscript
+var t := CFFI.find_type("int32_t")
+var p := t.alloc_array(3)
+p.set_value(10, 0)
+p.set_value(20, 2)
+print(p.get_value(0), " ", p.get_value(2))   # 10 20
+```
+
+## Address and element type
+
+```
+int     get_address()
+CFFIType get_element_type()
+```
+
+`get_address` is the raw address as an integer, which is what you pass to `CFFI.get_function_at`.
+
+```gdscript
+print(p.get_address())          # e.g. 140234567890176
+print(p.get_element_type().get_name())   # sint32
+```
+
+## Pointer arithmetic
+
+```
+CFFIPointer offset_by(int64_t offset)
+CFFIPointer cast_elements(Variant element_type)
+```
+
+`offset_by` counts elements of the current type. `cast_elements` reinterprets the same memory with a
+different element type — useful for treating a buffer as bytes or as a struct.
+
+```gdscript
+var bytes := CFFI.find_type("uint8").alloc_array(16).get_data()
+var ints := bytes.cast_elements("int32_t")
+print(ints.get_value(0))
+```
+
+## Copying
+
+```
+CFFIOwnedValue duplicate()
+CFFIOwnedArray duplicate_array(int64_t size)
+```
+
+`duplicate` copies a single element into newly owned memory. `duplicate_array` copies `size`
+elements and returns an owning span, which is how you keep data that C only borrowed.
+
+```gdscript
+var copy := p.duplicate_array(3)    # independent of p
+copy.set_value(99, 0)
+print(p.get_value(0))               # unchanged
+```
+
+## Strings
+
+```
+String get_string_from_ascii(int length = -1)
+String get_string_from_utf8(int length = -1)
+String get_string_from_utf16(int length = -1)
+String get_string_from_utf32(int length = -1)
+String get_string_from_wchar(int length = -1)
+```
+
+Decodes the pointed-to memory as text. With the default `length = -1` it reads until the terminating
+zero; pass a length to read a fixed number of code units.
+
+```gdscript
+var getenv: CFFIFunction = libc.get_function("getenv", "char*", ["char*"])
+var value: CFFIPointer = getenv.invokev([CFFI.null_terminated_utf8_buffer("HOME")])
+if value != null:
+    print(value.get_string_from_utf8())
+```
+
+## Arrays and dictionaries
+
+```
+Array to_array(int64_t length)
+PackedByteArray      to_byte_array(int64_t length)
+PackedInt32Array     to_int32_array(int64_t length)
+PackedInt64Array     to_int64_array(int64_t length)
+PackedFloat32Array   to_float32_array(int64_t length)
+PackedFloat64Array   to_float64_array(int64_t length)
+PackedVector2Array   to_vector2_array(int64_t length)
+PackedVector3Array   to_vector3_array(int64_t length)
+PackedVector4Array   to_vector4_array(int64_t length)
+PackedColorArray     to_color_array(int64_t length)
+Dictionary           to_dictionary()
+```
+
+The typed conversions copy `length` elements into the matching Godot type, which is the fast way to
+get C data into the engine. `to_dictionary` reads a struct's fields by name and returns them as a
+`Dictionary`.
+
+```gdscript
+var samples := CFFI.find_type("float").alloc_array(256)
+# ... fill one entry per sample ...
+var packed: PackedFloat32Array = samples.to_float32_array(256)
+```
+
+## Struct fields
+
+```
+CFFIPointer get_field(String field_name)
+```
+
+Returns a pointer to a field inside a struct instance, offset according to the struct's layout.
+
+```gdscript
+var v := CFFI.define_struct("vec2", {"x": "float", "y": "float"}).alloc()
+v.get_field("x").set_value(1.5)
+v.get_field("y").set_value(-1.5)
+print(v.to_dictionary())    # {x: 1.5, y: -1.5}
+```
+
+# CFFIOwnedValue
+
+A `CFFIPointer` that owns the single value it refers to and frees it when the last reference is
+released. This is what `CFFIType.alloc` and `CFFIPointer.duplicate` return. It adds no methods; the
+point is lifetime.
