index 00000000000..9a2733be84b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/type-strings.md
@@ -0,0 +1,65 @@
+---
+title: Type strings
+---
+
+# Type strings
+
+Wherever a type is expected you can pass a **string**, a `CFFIType` object, or (for structural
+types) a `Dictionary` of fields. Type strings are parsed with the same rules every time.
+
+## Base names
+
+Built in, available without declaring anything:
+
+| group | names |
+|---|---|
+| void | `void` |
+| signed integers | `sint8` `sint16` `sint32` `sint64`, `int8_t` … `int64_t`, `char`, `short`, `int`, `long`, `long long` |
+| unsigned integers | `uint8` `uint16` `uint32` `uint64`, `uint8_t` … `uint64_t`, `unsigned char`, `unsigned short`, `unsigned int` |
+| floating point | `float`, `double`, `long double` |
+| complex | `complex float`, `complex double`, `complex longdouble` |
+| others | `bool`, `pointer`, `size_t`, `ssize_t`, `intptr_t`, `uintptr_t`, `char16_t`, `char32_t` |
+
+The libffi spellings (`uint8`, `sint32`, …) and the `_t` spellings (`uint8_t`, …) are separate
+names that resolve to the same type.
+
+## Pointers and arrays
+
+```
+"int"        int
+"int *"      pointer to int
+"int **"     pointer to pointer to int
+"int[4]"     array of 4 int
+"int[3][4]"  array of 3 arrays of 4 int
+"int[ 4 ]"   whitespace inside the brackets is fine
+"int[]"      a POINTER, not a zero-length array
+"int[0]"     a zero-length (flexible) array member
+```
+
+`int[]` meaning pointer is deliberate: it is how C function signatures spell an unsized array
+parameter.
+
+## Qualifiers
+
+`const` and `volatile` are accepted as prefixes and ignored for layout:
+
+```
+"const int"
+"volatile int *"
+"const char **"
+```
+
+A trailing qualifier (`"int *const"`) does not parse. `const` and `volatile` are matched as bare
+prefixes without a word boundary, so a name beginning with those letters can be misread — `"constant"`
+parses as the identifier `ant`. Declare such names with a distinct spelling.
+
+## Structs
+
+Declare a struct once, then use its name:
+
+```gdscript
+var vec3 := CFFI.define_struct("vec3", {"x": "float", "y": "float", "z": "float"})
+var length: CFFIType = CFFI.find_type("vec3")
+```
+
+Or pass a `Dictionary` where a type is accepted, which defines the struct inline.
diff --git a/docs/types.md b/docs/types.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000000..5ffc2bca87f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/types.md
@@ -0,0 +1,98 @@
+---
+title: CFFIType
+---
+
+# CFFIType
+
+A resolved C type. Get one from [CFFI.find_type](cffi#find_type), or from
+[CFFI.define_struct](cffi#define_struct). It knows its size, its alignment, and how to allocate
+memory for itself.
+
+```gdscript
+var int_type := CFFI.find_type("int")
+print(int_type.get_name())        # int
+print(int_type.get_size())        # 4
+print(int_type.get_alignment())   # 4
+```
+
+## Methods
+
+```
+String  get_name()
+int64_t get_size()
+int64_t get_alignment()
+CFFIOwnedValue alloc(bool initialize_with_zeros = true)
+CFFIOwnedArray alloc_array(int64_t length, bool initialize_with_zeros = true)
+```
+
+`alloc` returns a pointer to one newly allocated value; `alloc_array` returns a span over `length`
+values. Both own their memory and free it when the last reference goes away, so you do not call
+`free` yourself.
+
+```gdscript
+var t := CFFI.find_type("double")
+var one := t.alloc()
+one.set_value(3.5)
+print(one.get_value())        # 3.5
+
+var many := t.alloc_array(4)  # zeroed
+many.set_value(1, 0)
+print(many.get_length())      # 4
+print(many.to_array())        # [1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]
+```
+
+# CFFIStructType
+
+A struct type. `define_struct` returns one.
+
+```
+CFFIType type_of(String field_name)
+int64_t  offset_of(String field_name)
+```
+
+```gdscript
+var vec3 := CFFI.define_struct("vec3", {"x": "float", "y": "float", "z": "float"})
+print(vec3.get_size())          # 12
+print(vec3.type_of("y").get_name())   # float
+print(vec3.offset_of("z"))      # 8
+
+var v := vec3.alloc()
+v.get_field("x").set_value(1.0)
+v.get_field("z").set_value(-2.0)
+print(v.get_field("z").get_value())   # -2.0
+```
+
+Layout follows C rules for the target platform, including padding, so a struct defined here matches
+the same struct in C.
+
+# CFFIArrayType
+
+An array type, produced by a type string such as `"int[4]"`.
+
+```
+CFFIType get_element_type()
+int64_t  get_length()
+static CFFIArrayType from(Variant type, int64_t length)
+```
+
+```gdscript
+var arr := CFFIArrayType.from("float", 3)
+print(arr.get_length())                  # 3
+print(arr.get_element_type().get_name()) # float
+print(arr.get_size())                    # 12
+```
+
+# CFFIPointerType
+
+A pointer type, produced by a type string such as `"char*"`.
+
+```
+CFFIType get_element_type()
+static CFFIPointerType from(Variant type)
+```
+
+```gdscript
+var p := CFFIPointerType.from("char")
+print(p.get_size())                      # 8 on 64-bit
+print(p.get_element_type().get_name())   # char
+```
